The Java Collections Framework (JCF) is a unified architecture in Java composed of a set of built-in interfaces, classes, and algorithms designed to store, manage, and manipulate groups of objects efficiently. Located in the java.util package, it serves as a pre-built "data structure toolbox" so developers do not have to write custom logic for standard operations like dynamic resizing, sorting, or searching.



Key Technical Differences

1. Synchronization and Performance

• Vector synchronizes almost every individual operation (add(), get(), remove(), etc.). This means if multiple threads try to access it, they must wait in a queue for object locks, introducing massive performance overhead.
• ArrayList does not implement synchronization. Because it avoids thread-locking delays, it executes much faster in single-threaded environments.

2. Memory Growth Footprint

• When an ArrayList fills up, its backing array grows dynamically by 50% of its original size (New Capacity = Current Capacity * 1.5).
• When a Vector fills up, it doubles its backing array size (100% increase). While this means Vector triggers fewer resizes during massive insertions, it wastes significantly more memory if the list stops expanding right after a resize event.

3. Modern Multi-Threaded Alternatives

If you require thread safety in a multi-threaded system, utilizing Vector is still generally discouraged today. Instead, developers use modern concurrency utilities:
• For read-heavy operations with low writing frequency, use java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList.
• To quickly turn an existing ArrayList thread-safe, wrap it via Collections.synchronizedList().